  "contracts": {
    "lem-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-adjoint-and-graph-core-lemma": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a real or complex inner-product space ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), with inner\nproduct linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the second, and\nwith the induced length $\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle}$. The definitions below\ndo not require completeness. When the term *Hilbert basis* is used, $H$ is\nadditionally assumed complete ([[def-hilbert-space]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$(e_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an orthonormal family in a real or complex Hilbert space $H$\n([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]), and\nfor $x\\in H$ and finite $F\\subseteq I$ put\n\n$$P_Fx:=\\sum_{i\\in F}\\langle x,e_i\\rangle e_i .$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-adjoint-of-a-densely-defined-unbounded-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $T$ be a\ndensely defined linear operator on $H$, with domain $D(T)$ dense in $H$\n([[def-densely-defined-closed-and-closable-operator]], [[def-dense-top]]).\nSince $D(T)$ is dense, and since $H$ is complete ([[def-hilbert-space]]), every\nbounded linear functional on the normed space $(D(T),\\|\\cdot\\|)$\n([[def-bounded-linear-operator",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-symmetric-self-adjoint-and-essentially-self-adjoint",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $T$ be a densely defined linear operator on $H$.\n\n- $T$ is **symmetric** when $T\\subseteq T^*$, that is, $D(T)\\subseteq D(T^*)$ and\n  $Tx=T^*x$ for all $x\\in D(T)$; equivalently\n  $\\langle Tx,y\\rangle=\\langle x,Ty\\rangle$ for all $x,y\\in D(T)$.\n- $T$ is **self-adjoint** when $T=T^*$, that is, the domains and the values",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-unbounded-adjoint-is-well-defined-and-closed",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For a densely defined linear operator $T$ on $H$ the\nadjoint $T^*$ is well defined and linear with linear domain $D(T^*)$; $T^*$ is\nclosed; for every $z\\in\\mathbb C$\n$$\\operatorname{ran}(T-z)^\\perp=\\ker(T^*-\\overline z),$$\nand if $S\\subseteq T$ and both are densely defined, then $T^*\\subseteq S^*$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-closable-iff-adjoint-domain-is-dense",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $T$ be a densely defined linear operator on $H$.\nThen $T$ is closable if and only if $D(T^*)$ is dense in $H$. In that case\n$\\overline T=T^{**}$ and $T^*=\\overline T^{\\,*}$; moreover if $T\\subseteq T^*$,\nthen $T$ is closable.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For vectors $u,v$ in a real or complex inner product space,\n\n$$|\\langle u,v\\rangle|\\le\\lVert u\\rVert\\lVert v\\rVert.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if $u$ and $v$ are linearly dependent, including the case in which either vector is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "[Well-definedness and density] $D_0$ is the linear span of the family and is a linear subspace; by [F1] the coefficients in a finite expansion are unique, so $S$ is well defined, and it is linear and maps $D_0$ into $H$. By [F1] the finite partial sums of the expansion of any $x\\in H$ converge to $x$, so the span $D_0$ is dense in $H$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "[Symmetry and the series for $A$] Let $x=\\sum_{n\\in F}c_ne_n$ and $y=\\sum_{m\\in G}d_me_m$ lie in $D_0$, with $c_n=\\langle x,e_n\\rangle$ and $d_m=\\langle y,e_m\\rangle$ by [F1]. Then $\\langle Sx,y\\rangle=\\sum_{n\\in F}\\lambda_nc_n\\overline{d_n}=\\sum_{n\\in F}c_n\\overline{\\lambda_nd_n}=\\langle x,Sy\\rangle$ because $\\lambda_n$ is real: $S$ is symmetric. For $x\\in D(A)$ the sequence $(\\lambda_n\\langle x,e_n\\rangle)_{n}$ is square summable with $\\sum_n\\lambda_n^2|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2<\\infty$, so its finite partial sums are Cauchy: $\\bigl\\|\\sum_{m\\le M<N}\\lambda_m\\langle x,e_m\\rangle e_m\\bigr\\|^2=\\sum_{M<m\\le N}\\lambda_m^2|\\langle x,e_m\\rangle|^2\\to0$ by [F1]; by completeness of $H$ the series $\\sum_n\\lambda_n\\langle x,e_n\\rangle e_n$ converges, its sum $Ax$ satisfies $\\|Ax\\|^2=\\sum_n\\lambda_n^2|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2$ (the finite-subset net of squared norms has the same supremum), and $A$ is linear because coefficient extraction is linear. If moreover $x\\in D_0$, then $\\langle x,e_n\\rangle=0$ for all but finitely many $n$ and $Ax=Sx$; hence $S\\subseteq A$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "[$S^*=A$] Let $y\\in D(S^*)$ and $w:=S^*y$. For each $n$, $\\lambda_n\\overline{\\langle y,e_n\\rangle}=\\langle Se_n,y\\rangle=\\langle e_n,w\\rangle=\\overline{\\langle w,e_n\\rangle}$, so $\\lambda_n\\langle y,e_n\\rangle=\\langle w,e_n\\rangle$. By [F1] applied to $w$, $\\sum_n\\lambda_n^2|\\langle y,e_n\\rangle|^2=\\sum_n|\\langle w,e_n\\rangle|^2=\\|w\\|^2<\\infty$, so $y\\in D(A)$; and $\\sum_n\\lambda_n\\langle y,e_n\\rangle e_n=\\sum_n\\langle w,e_n\\rangle e_n=w$ by [F1], so $S^*y=Ay$. Hence $S^*\\subseteq A$. Conversely let $y\\in D(A)$ and $x=\\sum_{n\\in F}c_ne_n\\in D_0$; then $\\langle Sx,y\\rangle=\\sum_{n\\in F}\\lambda_nc_n\\overline{\\langle y,e_n\\rangle}=\\langle x,Ay\\rangle$, so $x\\mapsto\\langle Sx,y\\rangle$ is the restriction to the dense subspace $D_0$ of the bounded functional $\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,,Ay\\rangle$ and is therefore bounded on $(D_0,\\|\\cdot\\|)$; by [F2] $y\\in D(S^*)$ and $S^*y=Ay$. Thus $S^*=A$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "[$A$ is self-adjoint] Let $z\\in D(A^*)$ and $v:=A^*z$. For $y\\in D(A)$ the pairing $\\langle Ay,z\\rangle=\\sum_n\\lambda_n\\langle y,e_n\\rangle\\overline{\\langle z,e_n\\rangle}$ is absolutely convergent by [F4] and $\\|Ay\\|\\,\\|z\\|$. Testing $y=e_m$ gives $\\lambda_m\\langle e_m,z\\rangle=\\langle Ae_m,z\\rangle=\\langle e_m,v\\rangle$, that is $\\lambda_m\\langle z,e_m\\rangle=\\langle v,e_m\\rangle$. By [F1], $\\sum_m\\lambda_m^2|\\langle z,e_m\\rangle|^2=\\sum_m|\\langle v,e_m\\rangle|^2=\\|v\\|^2<\\infty$, so $z\\in D(A)$; and then $Az=\\sum_m\\lambda_m\\langle z,e_m\\rangle e_m=\\sum_m\\langle v,e_m\\rangle e_m=v=A^*z$ by [F1]. Hence $D(A^*)\\subseteq D(A)$; the reverse inclusion was proved in step 1.3 as $A\\subseteq S^*$ with $S^*=A$, and directly: for $z\\in D(A)$ and $y\\in D(A)$, $\\langle Ay,z\\rangle=\\sum_n\\lambda_n\\langle y,e_n\\rangle\\overline{\\langle z,e_n\\rangle}=\\langle y,Az\\rangle$ by absolute convergence and realness of $\\lambda_n$, so $z\\in D(A^*)$ with $A^*z=Az$. Therefore $A^*=A$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.3",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "[Graph core] Let $x\\in D(A)$ and $x_N:=\\sum_{n=0}^N\\langle x,e_n\\rangle e_n\\in D_0\\subseteq D(A)$. By [F1], $\\|x-x_N\\|^2=\\sum_{n>N}|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2\\to0$, and by step 1.2, $\\|Ax-Ax_N\\|^2=\\sum_{n>N}\\lambda_n^2|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2\\to0$ because the series converges. Thus $(x_N,Ax_N)\\to(x,Ax)$ in $H\\oplus H$; since $x\\in D(A)$ was arbitrary, $D_0$ is dense in $(D(A),\\|\\cdot\\|_A)$, that is, $D_0$ is a core for $A$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "[Closure, essential self-adjointness and uniqueness] Since $S$ is symmetric, [F2] makes $S$ closable with symmetric closure $\\overline S$, and [F3] gives $\\overline S=S^{**}$. Since $A=S^*$ is the adjoint of a densely defined operator, $A$ is closed by [F2]; as $S\\subseteq A$, the least closed extension satisfies $\\overline S\\subseteq A$. Conversely, for $x\\in D(A)$ step 1.2 supplies $x_N\\in D_0$ with $x_N\\to x$ and $Sx_N=Ax_N\\to Ax$, so $(x,Ax)\\in\\overline{\\Gamma(S)}=\\Gamma(\\overline S)$ and $A\\subseteq\\overline S$. Hence $\\overline S=A$, and $S$ is essentially self-adjoint because $\\overline S=A$ is self-adjoint. If $T$ is any self-adjoint extension of $S$, then $T$ is closed and contains $\\overline S=A$; since $A$ is self-adjoint and $A\\subseteq T$ with $T$ symmetric, [F2] forces $T=A$. So $A$ is the unique self-adjoint extension of $S$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "[Positivity and eigenvalues] For $x\\in D(A)$ the series $\\sum_n\\lambda_n|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2$ is absolutely convergent by [F4] and step 1.2: $\\sum_n|\\lambda_n||\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2\\le(\\sum_n\\lambda_n^2|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2)^{1/2}(\\sum_n|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2)^{1/2}<\\infty$, and $\\langle Ax,x\\rangle=\\sum_n\\lambda_n|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2$ by continuity of the inner product in the first argument applied to the convergent series of step 1.2. Since the $\\lambda_n$ are real the value is real, and $\\lambda_n\\ge c$ for all $n$ gives $\\langle Ax,x\\rangle\\ge c\\|x\\|^2$ by [F1]. Finally $Ae_n=\\lambda_ne_n$ directly from the definition of $A$ and [F1], so each $\\lambda_n$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ with eigenvector $e_n\\ne0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "F1",
            "F4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
