    "thm-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-spectrum": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-adjoint-and-graph-core-lemma",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) with inner product linear in the first argument, let $(e_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}$, where $\\mathbb N=\\{0,1,2,\\dots\\}$, be a complete orthonormal family of $H$ ([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]), and let $(\\lambda_n)_{n\\in\\mathbb N}\\subseteq\\m",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$(e_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an orthonormal family in a real or complex Hilbert space $H$\n([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]), and\nfor $x\\in H$ and finite $F\\subseteq I$ put\n\n$$P_Fx:=\\sum_{i\\in F}\\langle x,e_i\\rangle e_i .$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-resolvent-of-a-closed-operator",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a Banach space over $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ ([[def-banach-space]], [[rem-real-and-complex-normed-space-convention]]) and let $A:D(A)\\subseteq X\\to X$ be a closed linear operator: $D(A)$ is a linear subspace and $\\Gamma(A)=\\{(x,Ax):x\\in D(A)\\}$ is closed in $X\\times X$ with norm $\\|(x,y)\\|=\\|x\\|+\\|y\\|$. This extends the Hilbert-space vocabulary of [[def-unbounded-linear-ope",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-cauchy-schwarz-for-real-and-complex-inner-product-spaces",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For vectors $u,v$ in a real or complex inner product space,\n\n$$|\\langle u,v\\rangle|\\le\\lVert u\\rVert\\lVert v\\rVert.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if $u$ and $v$ are linearly dependent, including the case in which either vector is zero.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "[Eigenvalues are spectral] By [F1], $Ae_n=\\lambda_ne_n$ with $e_n\\ne0$, so $\\lambda_n$ is an eigenvalue of $A$. If $\\lambda_n\\in\\rho(A)$, then $\\lambda_nI-A$ is injective by [F2], contradicting $(\\lambda_nI-A)e_n=0$. Hence $\\{\\lambda_n:n\\in\\mathbb N\\}\\subseteq\\sigma(A)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "[The resolvent outside the closure] Let $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb C$ with $d=\\inf_n|\\lambda-\\lambda_n|>0$, and define $Rx:=\\sum_n(\\lambda-\\lambda_n)^{-1}\\langle x,e_n\\rangle e_n$ for $x\\in H$. The series converges and $\\|Rx\\|^2=\\sum_n|\\lambda-\\lambda_n|^{-2}|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2\\le d^{-2}\\|x\\|^2$ by [F1], so $R\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ with $\\|R\\|\\le d^{-1}$; testing on $e_n$ gives $\\|R\\|\\ge\\sup_n|\\lambda-\\lambda_n|^{-1}=d^{-1}$, so $\\|R\\|=d^{-1}$. For $x\\in H$, the coefficients of $Rx$ satisfy $\\sum_n\\lambda_n^2|(\\lambda-\\lambda_n)^{-1}\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2\\le(1+|\\lambda|d^{-1})^2\\sum_n|\\langle x,e_n\\rangle|^2<\\infty$ because $|\\lambda_n|\\le|\\lambda-\\lambda_n|+|\\lambda|\\le|\\lambda-\\lambda_n|(1+|\\lambda|d^{-1})$ for every $n$; hence $Rx\\in D(A)$ by [F1]. Computing coefficients with [F1] gives $(\\lambda I-A)Rx=\\sum_n\\langle x,e_n\\rangle e_n=x$, and symmetrically $R(\\lambda I-A)x=\\sum_n(\\lambda-\\lambda_n)^{-1}(\\lambda-\\lambda_n)\\langle x,e_n\\rangle e_n=x$ for $x\\in D(A)$. So $\\lambda I-A$ is bijective with bounded inverse $R$, that is, $\\lambda\\in\\rho(A)$ and $R(\\lambda,A)=R$ by [F2].",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "[Closure points are spectral] Let $\\lambda\\in\\overline{\\{\\lambda_n\\}}$. If $\\lambda=\\lambda_m$ for some $m$, then $\\lambda\\in\\sigma(A)$ by step 1.1. Otherwise pick a subsequence with $\\lambda_{n_k}\\to\\lambda$ and $\\lambda_{n_k}\\ne\\lambda$ for all $k$, and suppose for contradiction that $\\lambda\\in\\rho(A)$. Then $1=\\|e_{n_k}\\|=\\|R(\\lambda,A)(\\lambda I-A)e_{n_k}\\|=\\|R(\\lambda,A)(\\lambda-\\lambda_{n_k})e_{n_k}\\|\\le\\|R(\\lambda,A)\\|\\,|\\lambda-\\lambda_{n_k}|\\to0$, which is impossible. Hence $\\lambda\\in\\sigma(A)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "[Conclusion] Steps 1.1 and 2.1 give $\\overline{\\{\\lambda_n\\}}\\subseteq\\sigma(A)$, and step 1.2 gives $\\mathbb C\\setminus\\overline{\\{\\lambda_n\\}}\\subseteq\\rho(A)$; taking complements yields $\\sigma(A)\\subseteq\\overline{\\{\\lambda_n\\}}$. Hence $\\sigma(A)=\\overline{\\{\\lambda_n\\}}$, and the equivalence \"$\\lambda\\in\\rho(A)$ exactly when $\\inf_n|\\lambda-\\lambda_n|>0$\" together with the resolvent formula and its norm are exactly the contents of step 1.2. For (c): if $\\lambda\\in\\sigma(A)\\setminus\\{\\lambda_n\\}$ and $\\lambda\\in\\overline{\\{\\lambda_n\\}}$ by the proven equality, a subsequence with $\\lambda_{n_k}\\to\\lambda$ satisfies $\\|(\\lambda I-A)e_{n_k}\\|=|\\lambda-\\lambda_{n_k}|\\to0$ with $\\|e_{n_k}\\|=1$; this is what \"approximate eigenvalue\" means here, and it is consistent with $\\ker(\\lambda I-A)=\\{0\\}$ because a nonzero kernel would only give a further eigenvector. No claim is made that such a $\\lambda$ is or is not an eigenvalue, and no spectral-type classification is asserted.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
