    "thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space.\n\n- For $0<p<\\infty$, define an equivalence relation on $\\mathcal L^p(\\mu)$ by\n  $$f\\sim g\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad f-g\\in\\mathcal N(\\mu),$$\n  and write $L^p(\\mu)$ for the set of classes $[f]$.\n- For $p=\\infty$, define the same relation on $L^\\infty(\\mu)$ and again write",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space. A finite-valued function $f=u+iv:X\\to\\mathbb C$ is **measurable** when $u,v$ are real measurable functions. Write\n$$N_p(f)=\\begin{cases}\\left(\\int_X|f|^p\\,d\\mu\\right)^{1/p},&\\displaystyle\\int_X|f|^p\\,d\\mu<\\infty,\\\\+\\infty,&\\displaystyle\\int_X|f|^p\\,d\\mu=+\\infty,\\end{cases}\\quad(1\\le p<\\infty),\\qquad N_\\infty(f)=\\inf\\{M\\in[0,\\infty]:|f|\\le M\\text{ a.e.}\\",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f,g\\in\\mathcal L^2(\\mu)$, then\n\n$$\\int |fg|\\,d\\mu\\le\\|f\\|_2\\|g\\|_2.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $f,g$ is zero almost everywhere,\nor there is a constant $c>0$ with",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-complex-gaussian-continuation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). For $z\\in\\mathbb C$ with $\\operatorname{Re}z>0$ and $n\\ge1$ let\n\n$$G_n(z):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-z|x|^2}\\,dx,$$\n\nunderstood as the absolutely convergent improper multiple integral of the real and imaginary parts ([[def-improper-integral-at-infinity]]). Then:",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-nonnegative-improper-riemann-integral-agrees-with-the-lebesgue-integral-on-a-half-line",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $a\\in\\mathbb R$ and let\n$f:[a,\\infty)\\to[0,\\infty)$ be Riemann integrable on every compact interval\n$[a,R]$ with $R>a$. If the improper Riemann integral\n$$\\int_a^\\infty f(x)\\,dx$$\nconverges in the sense of [[def-improper-integral-at-infinity]], then $f$ is\nLebesgue integrable on $[a,\\infty)$ and",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and conseq",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $z\\in\\mathbb C$, the series $\\sum z^n/n!$ converges absolutely.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-derivative-of-exponential",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The real exponential function is $C^\\infty$, and for every $m\\in\\mathbb N$,\n$$\\exp^{(m)}=\\exp.$$\nIn particular $(\\exp)'=\\exp$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-chain-rule",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A, B \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ with\n$g[A] \\subseteq B$ and let $f : B \\to \\mathbb{R}$, so that the composite\n$f \\circ g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined. Let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of\n$A$ ([[def-limit-point-r]]) at which $g$ is differentiable\n([[def-derivative]]), put $b := g(c)$, and suppose $b$ is a limit point of $B$\nat which $f$ is differentiable. Then $f \\circ g",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-algebra-of-derivatives",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $A \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$, let $c \\in A$ be a limit point of $A$\n([[def-limit-point-r]]), let $f, g : A \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at $c$\n([[def-derivative]]) and let $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{R}$. Then:\n\n1. $f + g$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(f+g)'(c) = f'(c) + g'(c)$;\n2. $\\alpha f$ is differentiable at $c$ and $(\\alpha f)'(c) = \\alpha f'(c)$;",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "1.4",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-ftc-second-part",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a < b$ be reals, let $G : [a,b] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be differentiable at every\npoint of $[a,b]$ as a function on $[a,b]$ ([[def-derivative]]; at $a$ and $b$\nthis is the one-sided derivative), let $f := G'$, and suppose $f$ is integrable\non $[a,b]$ ([[def-darboux-integral]]). Then\n\n$$\\int_a^b f \\;=\\; G(b) - G(a) .$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "[The Gaussian moments] By [F2], $\\|g_0\\|^2=\\pi^{-1/2}\\int e^{-y^2}d\\lambda=\\pi^{-1/2}\\sqrt\\pi=1$, so $g_0\\in L^2$ and $\\|g_0\\|=1$. For $k\\in\\mathbb N$ and $y\\in\\mathbb R$, $|y^kg_0(y)|^2=\\pi^{-1/2}y^{2k}e^{-y^2}\\le\\pi^{-1/2}2^kk!\\,e^{-y^2/2}$ by [F3]; hence $\\|y^kg_0\\|^2\\le\\pi^{-1/2}2^kk!\\sqrt{2\\pi}<\\infty$ by [F2], so $y^kg_0\\in L^2$. Every $pg_0$ with $\\deg p=m$ is a finite linear combination of $y^0g_0,\\dots,y^mg_0$, so $pg_0\\in L^2$: $P\\subseteq L^2$ is a linear subspace.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "[Invariance of $P$ and $ag_0=0$] For a polynomial $p$, $Y(pg_0)=(yp)g_0$ has polynomial factor $yp$, and by [F4] $\\partial_y(pg_0)=(p'-yp)g_0$ has polynomial factor $p'-yp$; so both $Y$ and $\\partial_y$, hence $a=(Y+\\partial_y)/\\sqrt2$ and $a^\\dagger=(Y-\\partial_y)/\\sqrt2$, map $P$ into $P$. Since $g_0>0$ and $g_0'=-yg_0$ by [F4], $(Y+\\partial_y)g_0=yg_0+g_0'=0$, i.e. $ag_0=0$. All members of $P$ are represented by $C^\\infty$ functions by [F4].",
          "inputs": [
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "[Adjoint pairing] Let $\\varphi,\\psi\\in P$ and consider $F:=\\varphi\\overline\\psi$, which is $C^1$ with $F'=\\varphi'\\overline\\psi+\\varphi\\overline{\\psi'}$. By [F5], for every $R>0$, $\\int_{-R}^RF'=F(R)-F(-R)$; as $R\\to\\infty$ the boundary terms tend to $0$ by [F5], so $\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\varphi'\\overline\\psi=-\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\varphi\\overline{\\psi'}$. Therefore $\\langle a^\\dagger\\varphi,\\psi\\rangle=2^{-1/2}\\int(y\\varphi-\\varphi')\\overline\\psi=2^{-1/2}\\int\\varphi\\overline{y\\psi+\\psi'}=\\langle\\varphi,a\\psi\\rangle$, since $y$ is real and $\\overline{\\psi'}$ is the derivative of $\\overline{\\psi}$; the second identity follows by exchanging $\\varphi$ and $\\psi$ and conjugating.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "step": "1.4",
          "claim": "[Rodrigues polynomials] Define $H_n(y):=(-1)^ne^{y^2}\\frac{d^n}{dy^n}e^{-y^2}$; each $H_n$ is a polynomial because differentiating $e^{-y^2}$ produces $e^{-y^2}$ times a polynomial, and $H_0=1$, $H_1=2y$. Put $F_n:=e^{y^2}\\frac{d^n}{dy^n}e^{-y^2}$, so $H_n=(-1)^nF_n$. By the product rule, $F_{n+1}=e^{y^2}\\frac{d}{dy}\\bigl(e^{-y^2}F_n\\bigr)=-2yF_n+F_n'$, hence $H_{n+1}=2yH_n-H_n'$. We prove $H_n'=2nH_{n-1}$ for all $n\\ge1$ by strong induction: for $n=1$ this is $H_1'=2=2H_0$, and if it holds for all $1\\le j\\le n$, then differentiating the displayed relation at $n$ gives $H_{n+1}'=2H_n+2yH_n'-H_n''$, while the induction hypothesis gives $H_n'=2nH_{n-1}$ and $H_n''=2nH_{n-1}'=4n(n-1)H_{n-2}$, and the same relation at $n-1$ reads $H_n=2yH_{n-1}-H_{n-1}'$, so $2yH_{n-1}=H_n+2(n-1)H_{n-2}$; substituting, $H_{n+1}'=2H_n+2n\\bigl(H_n+2(n-1)H_{n-2}\\bigr)-4n(n-1)H_{n-2}=2(n+1)H_n$, which is the claim for $n+1$. Hence $H_n'=2nH_{n-1}$ for all $n\\ge1$, and the displayed relation becomes the three-term recurrence $H_{n+1}=2yH_n-2nH_{n-1}$ for $n\\ge1$. Induction on this recurrence gives that $H_n$ has degree $n$ with leading coefficient $2^n$ (the term $2yH_n$ contributes $2\\cdot2^n=2^{n+1}$ to $y^{n+1}$ and $-2nH_{n-1}$ only degree $n-1$) and that $H_n(-y)=(-1)^nH_n(y)$, true for $n=0,1$ and inherited.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "[Commutator] On any $C^\\infty$ function $\\varphi$, $2(aa^\\dagger-a^\\dagger a)=(Y+\\partial_y)(Y-\\partial_y)-(Y-\\partial_y)(Y+\\partial_y)$, and expanding with the product rule [F4] gives $(Y+\\partial_y)(Y-\\partial_y)=Y^2-Y\\partial_y+\\partial_yY-\\partial_y^2$ and $(Y-\\partial_y)(Y+\\partial_y)=Y^2+Y\\partial_y-\\partial_yY-\\partial_y^2$; subtracting gives $2(\\partial_yY-Y\\partial_y)=2I$ because $\\partial_y(Y\\varphi)-Y(\\partial_y\\varphi)=\\varphi$. Hence $[a,a^\\dagger]=I$ on $P$ by step 1.2.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "[Identification of $g_n$] We prove $g_n=H_ng_0/\\sqrt{2^nn!}$ by induction on $n$. For $n=0$ this is $g_0=H_0g_0$. Assume it holds for $n$; by step 1.2 and [F4], $(Y-\\partial_y)(H_ng_0)=(2yH_n-H_n')g_0=(2yH_n-2nH_{n-1})g_0=H_{n+1}g_0$ by step 1.4, so $g_{n+1}=a^\\dagger g_n/\\sqrt{n+1}=(Y-\\partial_y)H_ng_0/(\\sqrt2\\sqrt{n+1}\\sqrt{2^nn!})=H_{n+1}g_0/\\sqrt{2^{n+1}(n+1)!}$, which completes the induction.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "1.4",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "[Ladder relations] From $[a,a^\\dagger]=I$ on $P$ we prove $[a,(a^\\dagger)^n]=n(a^\\dagger)^{n-1}$ on $P$ for every $n\\ge0$: for $n=0$ both sides vanish, and if it holds for $n$ then $[a,(a^\\dagger)^{n+1}]=[a,(a^\\dagger)^n]a^\\dagger+(a^\\dagger)^n[a,a^\\dagger]=n(a^\\dagger)^{n-1}a^\\dagger+(a^\\dagger)^n=(n+1)(a^\\dagger)^n$, all evaluated on $P$, which $(a^\\dagger)^n$ and $a$ preserve by step 1.2. Since $ag_0=0$, for $n\\ge1$ we get $ag_n=[a,(a^\\dagger)^n]g_0/\\sqrt{n!}=n(a^\\dagger)^{n-1}g_0/\\sqrt{n!}=\\sqrt n\\,g_{n-1}$, while $ag_0=0$; by definition $a^\\dagger g_n=(a^\\dagger)^{n+1}g_0/\\sqrt{n!}=\\sqrt{n+1}\\,g_{n+1}$ for every $n$, hence $a^\\dagger ag_n=\\sqrt n\\,a^\\dagger g_{n-1}=ng_n$ and, using $aa^\\dagger=a^\\dagger a+I$ from step 2.1, $aa^\\dagger g_n=(n+1)g_n$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "[The $y$ and $\\partial_y$ actions] Since $Y=(a+a^\\dagger)/\\sqrt2$ and $\\partial_y=(a-a^\\dagger)/\\sqrt2$ on $P$ by the definitions, applying step 3.1 gives $\\sqrt2\\,yg_n=ag_n+a^\\dagger g_n=\\sqrt n\\,g_{n-1}+\\sqrt{n+1}\\,g_{n+1}$ and $\\sqrt2\\,g_n'=ag_n-a^\\dagger g_n=\\sqrt n\\,g_{n-1}-\\sqrt{n+1}\\,g_{n+1}$. All identities are in $L^2$, where equalities mean equality of classes, and the underlying functions agree pointwise because both sides are represented by continuous functions; the polynomial identities of step 1.4 are pointwise identities of polynomials.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.4",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
