        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-parseval-equivalences-for-a-complete-orthonormal-family",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let\n$(e_i)_{i\\in I}$ be an orthonormal family in a real or complex Hilbert space $H$\n([[def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis]]), and\nfor $x\\in H$ and finite $F\\subseteq I$ put\n\n$$P_Fx:=\\sum_{i\\in F}\\langle x,e_i\\rangle e_i .$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-orthonormal-family-complete-orthonormal-system-and-hilbert-basis",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a real or complex inner-product space ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), with inner\nproduct linear in the first argument and conjugate-linear in the second, and\nwith the induced length $\\|v\\|=\\sqrt{\\langle v,v\\rangle}$. The definitions below\ndo not require completeness. When the term *Hilbert basis* is used, $H$ is\nadditionally assumed complete ([[def-hilbert-space]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-cauchy-schwarz-inequality-for-l-two",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $f,g\\in\\mathcal L^2(\\mu)$, then\n\n$$\\int |fg|\\,d\\mu\\le\\|f\\|_2\\|g\\|_2.$$\n\nEquality holds if and only if at least one of $f,g$ is zero almost everywhere,\nor there is a constant $c>0$ with",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $L^2=L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)$ be the complex Lebesgue space with inner product $\\langle\\varphi,\\psi\\rangle=\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\varphi(y)\\overline{\\psi(y)}\\,dy$ linear in the first argument ([[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]], [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]]). Write $g_0$ for the function $g_0(y):=",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and $f\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, with the componentwise integral and quotient conventions of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]], [[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]] and [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]. Define\n$$\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\mathcal Ff(\\xi):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f(x)\\exp(-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi)\\,dx,\\qquad \\xi\\in\\mathbb R^n.$$\nHere $x\\cdot\\xi=\\sum_{j=1}",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "cor-uniqueness-of-the-l-one-fourier-transform",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. If $f,g\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$ and $\\widehat f=\\widehat g$, then $f=g$ almost everywhere. Equality of the transforms almost everywhere already suffices.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrightarrow0,$$\nand hence",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-complex-exponential",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\iota_{\\mathbb R}\\colon\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb R$ be the canonical-natural map\nof [[def-canonical-natural]], and let $j\\colon\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ be the real\nembedding $j(x)=x+i0$ from [[def-complex-numbers-and-arithmetic]]. For every\n$n\\in\\mathbb N$, the factorial $n!$ is nonzero by\n[[def-factorial-and-falling-factorial]], so\n$\\iota_{\\mathbb R}(n!)>0$ by [[lem-of-naturals-positive]] and conseq",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-complex-exponential-series-converges-everywhere",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $z\\in\\mathbb C$, the series $\\sum z^n/n!$ converges absolutely.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-nonnegative-series-bounded-partial-sums",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(a_k)$ be a sequence of reals with $a_k \\ge 0$ for every $k \\in \\mathbb{N}$,\nlet $s_n = \\sum_{k<n} a_k$ be its partial sums and let\n$S = \\{\\, s_n : n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\,\\}$ be the range of $(s_n)$ ([[def-series]]).\nThen:\n\n1. $(s_n)$ is nondecreasing ([[def-monotone-sequence]]) and $s_n \\ge 0$ for every",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-complex-gaussian-continuation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). For $z\\in\\mathbb C$ with $\\operatorname{Re}z>0$ and $n\\ge1$ let\n\n$$G_n(z):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}e^{-z|x|^2}\\,dx,$$\n\nunderstood as the absolutely convergent improper multiple integral of the real and imaginary parts ([[def-improper-integral-at-infinity]]). Then:",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "[Reduction to the moment criterion] Since $(g_n)$ is orthonormal by [F1], [F2] reduces completeness to the statement that no nonzero $f\\in L^2$ is orthogonal to every $g_n$; the expansion and Parseval identities are part of the same equivalence. The moment criterion (a) is proved first, and then applied.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "[Vanishing moments] Let $f\\in L^2$ satisfy $\\langle f,g_n\\rangle=0$ for all $n$. Since $H_n$ is real-valued, [F1] gives $0=\\langle f,g_n\\rangle=(2^nn!)^{-1/2}\\int f\\,\\overline{H_ng_0}=(2^nn!)^{-1/2}\\int f\\,H_n\\,g_0$, so $\\int fH_ng_0=0$ for every $n$. The polynomials $H_0,\\dots,H_m$ have degrees $0,\\dots,m$ with nonzero leading coefficients, so they form a basis of the polynomials of degree $\\le m$; hence $\\int fqg_0=0$ for every polynomial $q$. Writing $h:=fg_0$, we have $h\\in L^1$ with $\\|h\\|_1\\le\\|f\\|_2$ by [F3], and $\\int h\\,q=0$ for every polynomial $q$; in particular all moments $\\int h(y)y^jdy$ vanish. This reduces the criterion (a) to showing that $h$ vanishes.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "[The transform vanishes] Fix $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R$, write $w:=2\\pi\\xi y$ and $S_N(y):=\\sum_{j=0}^N(-2\\pi i\\xi y)^j/j!$. Then $S_N(y)\\to e^{-2\\pi i\\xi y}$ for every $y$ by [F5], and $|S_N(y)|\\le\\sum_{j=0}^N|2\\pi\\xi y|^j/j!\\le e^{2\\pi|\\xi||y|}=:G(y)$ by [F5]. Moreover $hG\\in L^1$: by [F3], $|h(y)|G(y)\\le\\|f\\|_2\\bigl(\\int G(y)^2g_0(y)^2dy\\bigr)^{1/2}$, and $G(y)^2g_0(y)^2=\\pi^{-1/2}e^{4\\pi|\\xi||y|-y^2}\\le\\pi^{-1/2}e^{8\\pi^2\\xi^2}e^{-y^2/2}$ because $4\\pi|\\xi||y|-y^2\\le8\\pi^2\\xi^2-|y|^2/2$; the last integral is finite by [F6]. Hence [F4] applies with $\\widehat h(\\xi)=\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\int hS_N=\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\sum_{j=0}^N\\frac{(-2\\pi i\\xi)^j}{j!}\\int h(y)y^jdy=0$, the first equality by dominated convergence, the interchange of the finite sum with the integral by linearity of the integral, and the final equality because all moments of $h$ vanish by step 1.2.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "[Conclusion of (a) and (b)] Since $\\widehat h=0$ identically, [F4] gives $h=fg_0=0$ a.e.; as $g_0(y)=\\pi^{-1/4}e^{-y^2/2}>0$ for every $y$, $f=0$ a.e. This proves (a), and with step 1.1 it proves that $(g_n)$ is complete. By [F2] applied to the complete orthonormal family $(g_n)$, every $f\\in L^2$ has the stated expansion and Parseval identity, which is (b).",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "F2",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "[Density of $P$] By [F2] and step 3.1 the closed linear span of $(g_n)$ is $L^2$. Since every $g_n\\in P$ by [F1] and $P$ is a linear subspace, $P$ contains the span of the family; a subspace containing a dense subspace is dense, so $P$ is dense. This is (c).",
          "inputs": [
            "3.1",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
