        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space. A finite-valued function $f=u+iv:X\\to\\mathbb C$ is **measurable** when $u,v$ are real measurable functions. Write\n$$N_p(f)=\\begin{cases}\\left(\\int_X|f|^p\\,d\\mu\\right)^{1/p},&\\displaystyle\\int_X|f|^p\\,d\\mu<\\infty,\\\\+\\infty,&\\displaystyle\\int_X|f|^p\\,d\\mu=+\\infty,\\end{cases}\\quad(1\\le p<\\infty),\\qquad N_\\infty(f)=\\inf\\{M\\in[0,\\infty]:|f|\\le M\\text{ a.e.}\\",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "[$D$ is a bijective isometry] For $h\\in L^1(\\mathbb R)$ the substitution $T(x)=\\ell x$, $T'(x)=\\ell>0$ in [F4] gives $\\int_{\\mathbb R}h(y)dy=\\ell\\int_{\\mathbb R}h(\\ell x)dx$, i.e. $\\int h(x/\\ell)dx=\\ell\\int h(y)dy$. Applying this to $h=|f|^2\\in L^1$ for $f\\in L^2$ gives $\\|Df\\|^2=\\ell^{-1}\\int|f(x/\\ell)|^2dx=\\|f\\|^2$; $D$ is linear, so it is a linear isometry. The map $D^{-1}g(y):=\\ell^{1/2}g(\\ell y)$ is linear and satisfies $(D(D^{-1}g))(x)=\\ell^{-1/2}\\ell^{1/2}g(x)=g(x)$ and $(D^{-1}(Df))(y)=\\ell^{1/2}\\ell^{-1/2}f(y)=f(y)$ as functions, hence as $L^2$ classes; so $D$ is a bijection, i.e. a unitary map.",
          "inputs": [
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "[Transport of the orthonormal basis] By step 1.1 and the definition $\\psi_n=Dg_n$, for all $m,n$ we have $\\langle\\psi_n,\\psi_m\\rangle_{L^2(dx)}=\\int\\ell^{-1}g_n(x/\\ell)\\overline{g_m(x/\\ell)}dx=\\int g_n(y)\\overline{g_m(y)}dy=\\delta_{nm}$ by the substitution of step 1.1 applied to $g_n\\overline{g_m}\\in L^1$ and [F1]. If $h\\in L^2(dx)$ satisfies $\\langle h,\\psi_n\\rangle=0$ for all $n$, then $\\langle D^{-1}h,g_n\\rangle=\\langle h,\\psi_n\\rangle=0$ for all $n$ because $D$ is an isometry, so $D^{-1}h=0$ by completeness of $(g_n)$ and $h=0$. Hence $(\\psi_n)$ is a complete orthonormal family.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "[$H_{\\mathrm{osc}}$ is the diagonal operator in the transported basis] Let $B$ be the diagonal operator of [[lem-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-adjoint-and-graph-core-lemma]] for the complete orthonormal family $(\\psi_n)$ and the eigenvalues $\\lambda_n'=\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)$, so $D(B)=\\{f:\\sum_n(\\lambda_n')^2|\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2<\\infty\\}$ and $Bf=\\sum_n\\lambda_n'\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle\\psi_n$. By that lemma and [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-diagonal-spectrum]], $B$ is self-adjoint with $\\sigma(B)=\\overline{\\{\\lambda_n'\\}}=\\{\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)\\}$ (a closed discrete set) and $\\langle Bf,f\\rangle\\ge\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}\\|f\\|^2$ because $\\lambda_n'\\ge\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}$. We claim $B=H_{\\mathrm{osc}}$ as operators. First, $\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle=\\langle D^{-1}f,g_n\\rangle$ by the isometry of step 1.1 applied to $f=D(D^{-1}f)$ and $\\psi_n=Dg_n$; hence $D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})=\\{f:D^{-1}f\\in D(A)\\}=D(B)$. Second, if $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ and $c_n=\\langle D^{-1}f,g_n\\rangle$, then $D^{-1}f\\in D(A)$ means $\\sum_n(2n+1)^2|c_n|^2<\\infty$, and the series $\\sum_n(2n+1)c_ng_n$ converges in $L^2$ to $AD^{-1}f$ by [F2]; since $D$ is a bijective isometry it preserves limits of series, so $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f=\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}D\\bigl(\\sum_n(2n+1)c_ng_n\\bigr)=\\sum_n\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)c_n\\psi_n=Bf$, and $\\sum_n(\\lambda_n')^2|c_n|^2=\\frac{\\hbar^2\\omega^2}{4}\\sum_n(2n+1)^2|c_n|^2<\\infty$. Hence $B=H_{\\mathrm{osc}}$, giving self-adjointness, the eigenvalue formulas $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}\\psi_n=\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)\\psi_n$, the spectrum, the domain formula, the ground energy $\\hbar\\omega/2$ and the lower bound.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "[The differential expression on the core] For a polynomial $p$ put $F:=pg_0\\in P$ and $f:=DF$, so $f(x)=\\ell^{-1/2}p(x/\\ell)g_0(x/\\ell)$. By the chain rule and product rule [F5], $f$ is $C^\\infty$ with $f'(x)=\\ell^{-3/2}F'(x/\\ell)$ and $f''(x)=\\ell^{-5/2}F''(x/\\ell)$. Since $\\hbar^2/(2m\\ell^2)=\\hbar\\omega/2$ and $m\\omega^2\\ell^2/2=\\hbar\\omega/2$ by $\\ell^2=\\hbar/(m\\omega)$, for all $x$ one has $-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}f''(x)+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2f(x)=\\ell^{-1/2}\\bigl(-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m\\ell^2}F''+\\frac{m\\omega^2\\ell^2}{2}y^2F\\bigr)\\big|_{y=x/\\ell}=\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}\\ell^{-1/2}(-F''+y^2F)(x/\\ell)$. By [F3], $-F''+y^2F=AF$ as functions on $\\mathbb R$, because the operator identity on $P$ holds for the smooth representatives; hence the right side equals $\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}(DAF)(x)=H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f(x)$ by step 3.1. This proves the pointwise identity. Moreover every $\\varphi\\in P$ is a finite combination of the $g_n$, so $D(P)=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\psi_n\\}$; since $P$ is a core for $A$ by [F2], its image under the unitary $D$ is a core for $DAD^{-1}=H_{\\mathrm{osc}}$: for $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ with $D^{-1}f\\in D(A)$ choose $\\varphi_k\\in P$ with $\\varphi_k\\to D^{-1}f$ and $A\\varphi_k\\to AD^{-1}f$; then $D\\varphi_k\\to f$ and $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}(D\\varphi_k)=\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}DA\\varphi_k\\to\\frac{\\hbar\\omega}{2}DAD^{-1}f=H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f$.",
          "inputs": [
            "3.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "step": "5.1",
          "claim": "[Weak identity for general elements] Let $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ and $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R)$; put $S\\varphi:=-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\varphi''+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2\\varphi$, an element of $L^2(\\mathbb R)$ by [F6]. By step 4.1 with the core $D(P)$, choose $f_k\\in D(P)$ with $f_k\\to f$ and $H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f_k\\to H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f$ in $L^2$. For each $k$, write $f_k=DF$ with $F\\in P$; by [F5] applied on $[-R,R]$ and the fact that $F$ is a polynomial times $g_0$, whose products with $\\varphi$ and their derivatives tend to $0$ at $\\pm\\infty$, integrating by parts twice gives $\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\bigl(-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}f_k''+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2f_k\\bigr)\\varphi\\,dx=\\int_{\\mathbb R}f_k\\bigl(-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m}\\varphi''+\\frac{m\\omega^2}{2}x^2\\varphi\\bigr)dx$. By step 4.1 the left side is $\\int(H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f_k)\\varphi$. Letting $k\\to\\infty$, [F6] and [F5] give $|\\int(H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f_k-H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f)\\varphi|\\le\\|H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f_k-H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f\\|_2\\|\\varphi\\|_2\\to0$ and $|\\int(f_k-f)S\\varphi|\\le\\|f_k-f\\|_2\\|S\\varphi\\|_2\\to0$, so the identity of the statement holds for $f$.",
          "inputs": [
            "4.1",
            "F5",
            "F6"
