          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $T$ be a\ndensely defined linear operator on $H$, with domain $D(T)$ dense in $H$\n([[def-densely-defined-closed-and-closable-operator]], [[def-dense-top]]).\nSince $D(T)$ is dense, and since $H$ is complete ([[def-hilbert-space]]), every\nbounded linear functional on the normed space $(D(T),\\|\\cdot\\|)$\n([[def-bounded-linear-operator",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-unbounded-adjoint-is-well-defined-and-closed",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For a densely defined linear operator $T$ on $H$ the\nadjoint $T^*$ is well defined and linear with linear domain $D(T^*)$; $T^*$ is\nclosed; for every $z\\in\\mathbb C$\n$$\\operatorname{ran}(T-z)^\\perp=\\ker(T^*-\\overline z),$$\nand if $S\\subseteq T$ and both are densely defined, then $T^*\\subseteq S^*$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
