          "claim": "[$(a^\\dagger)^*=a$ and closedness] Let $y\\in D((a^\\dagger)^*)$ and $w:=(a^\\dagger)^*y$; testing against $x=e_m$ with $m\\ge0$ gives $\\sqrt{m+1}\\langle y,e_{m+1}\\rangle=\\langle w,e_m\\rangle$, so $\\sum_{m\\ge0}(m+1)|\\langle y,e_{m+1}\\rangle|^2=\\sum_m|\\langle w,e_m\\rangle|^2<\\infty$, that is $\\sum_{n\\ge1}n|\\langle y,e_n\\rangle|^2<\\infty$; thus $y\\in D(a)$ and $ay=\\sum_{n\\ge1}\\sqrt n\\langle y,e_n\\rangle e_{n-1}=\\sum_{m\\ge0}\\langle w,e_m\\rangle e_m=w$. Conversely for $y\\in D(a)$ and $x\\in D(a^\\dagger)$ the same two absolutely convergent series show $\\langle a^\\dagger x,y\\rangle=\\langle x,ay\\rangle$, so $y\\in D((a^\\dagger)^*)$ with $(a^\\dagger)^*y=ay$. Hence $(a^\\dagger)^*=a$. Since $a=(a^\\dagger)^*$ and $a^\\dagger=a^*$ are adjoints of densely defined operators, [F2] makes both closed.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-4",
          "step": "1.4",
          "claim": "[Kernels] If $ax=0$ then $\\sqrt{m+1}c_{m+1}=0$ for all $m$, so $c_n=0$ for $n\\ge1$ and $x=c_0e_0$; conversely $ae_0=0$. If $a^\\dagger x=0$ then $\\sqrt m\\,c_{m-1}=0$ for all $m\\ge1$, so $c_n=0$ for all $n$ and $x=0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-5",
          "step": "1.5",
          "claim": "[Products, commutator and exact domain] For $x\\in H$ with coefficients $c_n$: $a^\\dagger x$ has coefficients $(a^\\dagger x)_m=\\sqrt m\\,c_{m-1}$ for $m\\ge1$ and $0$ for $m=0$, so $a(a^\\dagger x)$ has coefficients $m\\,c_{m-1}\\cdot$... computing directly, $(aa^\\dagger x)_n=(n+1)c_n$, and the product is defined exactly when $\\sum_m(m+1)^2|c_{m+1}|^2<\\infty$, that is $\\sum_n n^2|c_n|^2<\\infty$ up to the finite $n=0,1$ terms, i.e. exactly for $x\\in D(N)$. Similarly $(a^\\dagger ax)_n=nc_n$ with the product defined exactly for $x\\in D(N)$. Hence $D(aa^\\dagger)=D(a^\\dagger a)=D(N)$, on which $a^\\dagger a=N$ and $aa^\\dagger=N+I$; subtracting gives $[a,a^\\dagger]x=x$ for $x\\in D(N)$. A vector not in $D(N)$ has at least one of the two products undefined, so $D(N)$ is exactly the common domain of the two products.",
          "inputs": [
