        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "[(b) unitarity of $U(t)$] For $f\\in L^2$ the coefficients $c_n=\\langle f,\\psi_n\\rangle$ are square summable, so the series in (b) converges and $\\|U(t)f\\|^2=\\sum_n|c_n|^2=\\|f\\|^2$ by [F5]; $U(t)$ is linear and isometric. Its range contains every $\\psi_n=U(t)\\bigl(e^{i\\omega(n+1/2)t}\\psi_n\\bigr)$, hence is dense; an isometry has closed range, so $U(t)$ is surjective: it is a bijective isometry. Also $U(t)\\psi_n=e^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)t}\\psi_n$ shows $U(t)U(t)^*$ fixes every $\\psi_n$, hence equals the identity on the dense span; by continuity $U(t)^*=U(t)^{-1}$, and $U(t)^{-1}=U(-t)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-3",
          "step": "1.3",
          "claim": "[(d) the position and momentum operators] The conjugation claim of [F4]: if $T=T^*$ on $K$ and $U:H\\to K$ is a bijective isometry, let $T':=UTU^*$ on $D(T'):=UD(T)$. For $x,y\\in D(T')$, writing $x=U\\xi$, $y=U\\eta$ with $\\xi,\\eta\\in D(T)$, $\\langle T'x,y\\rangle=\\langle T\\xi,\\eta\\rangle=\\langle\\xi,T\\eta\\rangle=\\langle x,T'y\\rangle$, so $T'$ is symmetric. If $y\\in D((T')^*)$ then for all $\\xi\\in D(T)$, $\\langle T\\xi,U^*y\\rangle=\\langle T'U\\xi,y\\rangle=\\langle U\\xi,(T')^*y\\rangle=\\langle\\xi,U^*(T')^*y\\rangle$, so $U^*y\\in D(T^*)=D(T)$ and $T^*U^*y=U^*(T')^*y$; hence $y\\in UD(T)=D(T')$ and $(T')^*y=UTU^*y=T'y$: $D((T')^*)\\subseteq D(T')$ and $T'$ is self-adjoint. Applying this with $U=\\mathcal F_2$ and the real multiplier $T=M_{2\\pi\\hbar\\xi}$ (self-adjoint by [F4]) makes $P$ self-adjoint with the stated domain, and [F4] identifies $D(P)=W^{1,2}$ and $Pf=-i\\hbar f'$ there. The position operator $Q=M_x$ is self-adjoint by [F4]. For the basis vectors, $Q\\psi_n(x)=x\\ell^{-1/2}g_n(x/\\ell)=\\ell\\,D(yg_n)(x)=\\frac{\\ell}{\\sqrt2}D(\\sqrt ng_{n-1}+\\sqrt{n+1}g_{n+1})(x)$ by [F3], which is the first displayed formula with $\\psi_{-1}=0$; and $P\\psi_n=-i\\hbar\\psi_n'$ with $\\psi_n'=\\ell^{-1}D(g_n')=\\frac{\\ell^{-1}}{\\sqrt2}D(\\sqrt ng_{n-1}-\\sqrt{n+1}g_{n+1})$, giving the second formula.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "[(b) group and strong continuity] $U(t+s)f=\\sum_ne^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)(t+s)}c_n\\psi_n=U(t)U(s)f$ by absolute convergence of the two series and [F5]; $U(0)=I$. For continuity at $s$: $\\|U(t)f-U(s)f\\|^2=\\sum_n|e^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)t}-e^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)s}|^2|c_n|^2$, and for $t\\to s$ the summands converge to $0$ pointwise in $n$ while being bounded by $4|c_n|^2$, so the sum tends to $0$ (the tail $\\sum_{n>N}4|c_n|^2$ is arbitrarily small and finitely many summands converge).",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "[(c) evolution of coherent vectors] By the definition of $\\psi_z$ in the $(\\psi_n)$-basis, $U(t)\\psi_z=e^{-|z|^2/2}\\sum_n\\frac{z^n}{\\sqrt{n!}}e^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)t}\\psi_n=e^{-i\\omega t/2}\\cdot e^{-|z|^2/2}\\sum_n\\frac{(ze^{-i\\omega t})^n}{\\sqrt{n!}}\\psi_n=e^{-i\\omega t/2}\\psi_{ze^{-i\\omega t}}$, the series converging by step 1.2 and the definition of $\\psi_w$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "step": "2.3",
          "claim": "[(d),(e) action on coherent vectors and the moments] Since $\\psi_z\\in D(N)\\subseteq D(a^\\dagger)=D(a)\\cap D(a^\\dagger)$ and $a_\\psi,a^\\dagger_\\psi$ are (transports of) the closed operators of [F2], the operators $\\frac{\\ell}{\\sqrt2}(a_\\psi+a^\\dagger_\\psi)$ and $\\frac{\\hbar}{i\\ell\\sqrt2}(a_\\psi-a^\\dagger_\\psi)$ agree with $Q$ and $P$ on every $\\psi_n$ by step 1.3, and $Q,P$ are closed; the partial sums $\\psi_z^{(N)}=e^{-|z|^2/2}\\sum_{n\\le N}\\frac{z^n}{\\sqrt{n!}}\\psi_n$ converge to $\\psi_z$ and their $Q$-images converge: $\\|(a+a^\\dagger)(\\psi_z^{(N)}-\\psi_z)\\|^2\\le2(\\|a(\\cdot)\\|^2+\\|a^\\dagger(\\cdot)\\|^2)\\le4\\sum_{n>N}(n+1)|c_n|^2\\to0$, and likewise for $a-a^\\dagger$; hence the displayed identities for $Q\\psi_z$ and $P\\psi_z$ hold, and $Q\\psi_z,P\\psi_z\\in L^2$. Therefore $\\langle Q\\rangle_z=\\frac{\\ell}{\\sqrt2}(z+\\overline z)=\\sqrt2\\ell\\operatorname{Re}z$ and $\\langle P\\rangle_z=\\frac{\\hbar}{i\\ell\\sqrt2}(z-\\overline z)=\\frac{\\sqrt2\\hbar}{\\ell}\\operatorname{Im}z$. For the variances, $\\operatorname{Var}(Q)_z=\\|Q\\psi_z\\|^2-\\langle Q\\rangle_z^2$ because the mean is real, and $\\|Q\\psi_z\\|^2=\\frac{\\ell^2}{2}\\|(a+a^\\dagger)\\psi_z\\|^2=\\frac{\\ell^2}{2}\\bigl(\\|a\\psi_z\\|^2+\\|a^\\dagger\\psi_z\\|^2+2\\operatorname{Re}\\langle a\\psi_z,a^\\dagger\\psi_z\\rangle\\bigr)$. Now $\\langle a\\psi_z,a^\\dagger\\psi_z\\rangle=\\langle z\\psi_z,a^\\dagger\\psi_z\\rangle=z\\overline{\\langle a^\\dagger\\psi_z,\\psi_z\\rangle}=z^2$ because $\\langle a^\\dagger\\psi_z,\\psi_z\\rangle=\\langle\\psi_z,a\\psi_z\\rangle=\\overline z$; and $\\|a\\psi_z\\|^2=|z|^2$, $\\|a^\\dagger\\psi_z\\|^2=|z|^2+1$ by [F2]. So $\\|Q\\psi_z\\|^2=\\frac{\\ell^2}{2}(2|z|^2+1+2\\operatorname{Re}z^2)=\\frac{\\ell^2}{2}((z+\\overline z)^2+1)$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(Q)_z=\\frac{\\ell^2}{2}$. The same computation with a minus sign gives $\\|P\\psi_z\\|^2=\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2\\ell^2}(2|z|^2+1-2\\operatorname{Re}z^2)$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(P)_z=\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2\\ell^2}$ because $\\langle P\\rangle_z^2=\\frac{2\\hbar^2}{\\ell^2}(\\operatorname{Im}z)^2=\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2\\ell^2}(2|z|^2-2\\operatorname{Re}z^2)$; the product is $\\frac{\\ell^2}{2}\\cdot\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2\\ell^2}=\\frac{\\hbar^2}{4}$. No minimality among all states is asserted; the displayed values are those computed for the coherent vectors.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.3",
            "F2",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "[(b) generator and its exact domain] Let $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ and $g_t:=\\frac1t(U(t)f-f)+\\frac{i}{\\hbar}H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f=\\sum_n\\varepsilon_n(t)c_n\\psi_n$ with $\\varepsilon_n(t)=\\frac{e^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)t}-1}{t}+i\\omega(n+\\frac12)$. By [F5], $|\\varepsilon_n(t)|\\le2\\omega(n+\\frac12)$, so $\\sum_n|\\varepsilon_n(t)|^2|c_n|^2\\le4\\omega^2\\sum_n(n+\\frac12)^2|c_n|^2<\\infty$ by step 1.1, and $\\varepsilon_n(t)\\to0$ as $t\\to0$ for each $n$ by $e^{ix}=1+ix+o(x)$; the same dominated-convergence argument as in step 2.1 gives $\\|g_t\\|\\to0$. Hence $t\\mapsto U(t)f$ is differentiable at $0$ with derivative $-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f$; the general $t$ follows from the group law and $U(t)f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ (the coefficients $e^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)t}c_n$ have the same moduli as $c_n$). Conversely, if $\\frac1t(U(t)f-f)\\to g$ in $L^2$, then for every $n$, $\\langle\\frac1t(U(t)f-f),\\psi_n\\rangle=\\frac{e^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)t}-1}{t}c_n\\to-i\\omega(n+\\frac12)c_n$ while these pairings converge to $\\langle g,\\psi_n\\rangle$; hence $\\langle g,\\psi_n\\rangle=-i\\omega(n+\\frac12)c_n$ for every $n$ and $\\sum_n(n+\\frac12)^2|c_n|^2=\\omega^{-2}\\sum_n|\\langle g,\\psi_n\\rangle|^2=\\omega^{-2}\\|g\\|^2<\\infty$ by [F5], so $f\\in D(H_{\\mathrm{osc}})$ and $g=-\\frac{i}{\\hbar}H_{\\mathrm{osc}}f$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "step": "4.1",
          "claim": "[(f) evolved moments] By step 2.2 and the definitions, $\\langle Q\\rangle_{z(t)}=\\langle Q(e^{-i\\omega t/2}\\psi_{z(t)}),e^{-i\\omega t/2}\\psi_{z(t)}\\rangle=\\langle Q\\psi_{z(t)},\\psi_{z(t)}\\rangle=\\sqrt2\\ell\\operatorname{Re}z(t)$, and similarly $\\langle P\\rangle_{z(t)}=\\frac{\\sqrt2\\hbar}{\\ell}\\operatorname{Im}z(t)$; the variances in step 2.3 depend on $z$ only through the value of the displayed constant formulas, which are independent of $z$, so they are unchanged: $\\operatorname{Var}(Q)_{z(t)}=\\ell^2/2$ and $\\operatorname{Var}(P)_{z(t)}=\\hbar^2/(2\\ell^2)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "2.2",
            "2.3"
          ]
        }
      ],
