            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "[(a) product basis] Applying [F2] inductively, the elementary products are orthonormal in the completed tensor product and their closed span is the whole tensor product; transporting along the surjective linear isometry $\\Theta$ preserves this: for $N,M$, $\\langle g_N,g_M\\rangle=\\prod_j\\langle g^{(j)}_{n_j},g^{(j)}_{m_j}\\rangle=\\delta_{NM}$, and if $f\\perp g_N$ for all $N$ then $\\Theta^{-1}f$ is orthogonal to every product basis vector, hence $\\Theta^{-1}f=0$ and $f=0$; so $(g_N)$ is complete. The expansion and Parseval statement is [F3]/Parseval equivalences.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "[(b) dimensionless operator] By [F5] the level sets of $M\\mapsto M=\\sum_jn_j$ are finite and exhaustive; enumerate $\\mathbb N^d$ by listing the levels in increasing $M$ and each level in a fixed order, obtaining a bijection $\\varphi:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N^d$. Apply [F3] to $e_m:=g_{\\varphi(m)}$ and $\\lambda_m:=\\lambda_{\\varphi(m)}$: the resulting operator $A_d$ is self-adjoint with the stated domain and $A_dg_N=\\lambda_Ng_N$, and its spectrum is the closure of $\\{\\lambda_N:N\\in\\mathbb N^d\\}=\\{2M+d:M\\in\\mathbb N\\}$, which is closed because it is a discrete set of reals and its only accumulation point would be $+\\infty$; by the diagonal lemma the algebraic span of the $g_N$ is a core. This is (b), except the differential expression, proved in the next step.",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "[(b) differential expression] On a product vector, $-\\partial_{y_j}^2$ and multiplication by $y_j^2$ act only on the $j$-th factor: for $g_N=\\prod_jg^{(j)}_{n_j}$ one has $(-\\partial_{y_j}^2+y_j^2)g_N=\\bigl((-\\partial_{y_j}^2+y_j^2)g^{(j)}_{n_j}\\bigr)\\prod_{k\\ne j}g^{(k)}_{n_k}=(2n_j+1)g_N$ by [F1]. Summing over $j$ gives $\\sum_j(-\\partial_{y_j}^2+y_j^2)g_N=\\lambda_Ng_N=A_dg_N$; both sides are linear, so the identity extends to the span, which is the core of step 1.2.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.2",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "step": "2.2",
          "claim": "[(c) anisotropic operator] The transport $D_d=\\Theta(D_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes D_d)\\Theta^{-1}$ is a composition of bijective isometries, hence a bijective isometry, and $\\psi_N=D_dg_N=\\Theta(\\psi^{(1)}_{n_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes\\psi^{(d)}_{n_d})$, which is a complete orthonormal family by step 1.1 and [F4]. Apply [F3] to $(\\psi_N)$ and $E_N$: $H$ is self-adjoint with the displayed domain and spectrum (the closure of the set of eigenvalues, as asserted), $H\\psi_N=E_N\\psi_N$, and the span of the $\\psi_N$ is a core. On a single product, each one-dimensional summand acts in its own coordinate and, by [F4], $\\bigl(-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m_j}\\partial_{x_j}^2+\\frac{m_j\\omega_j^2}{2}x_j^2\\bigr)\\psi_N=\\hbar\\omega_j(n_j+\\frac12)\\psi_N$; summing over $j$ gives $\\sum_j\\bigl(-\\frac{\\hbar^2}{2m_j}\\partial_{x_j}^2+\\frac{m_j\\omega_j^2}{2}x_j^2\\bigr)\\psi_N=E_N\\psi_N=H\\psi_N$, and linearity extends the identity to the core.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "F3",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "[(d) isotropic multiplicities] Set $\\omega_j=\\omega$. Then $E_N=\\hbar\\omega(M+\\frac d2)$ with $M=\\sum_jn_j$, so $\\{E_N\\}=\\{\\hbar\\omega(M+\\frac d2):M\\in\\mathbb N\\}$, a closed discrete set; by step 2.2 the spectrum is its closure, which is itself. Since $(\\psi_N)$ is an orthonormal family and $H\\psi_N=E_N\\psi_N$, the eigenspace for $\\hbar\\omega(M+\\frac d2)$ contains the span of all $\\psi_N$ with $\\sum_jn_j=M$; conversely, any element of $D(H)$ orthogonal to that span has all its nonzero coefficients on $\\psi_N$ with $\\sum_jn_j\\ne M$, so by the diagonal form $Hf=\\sum_NE_N\\langle f,\\psi_N\\rangle\\psi_N$ of [F3] it lies in $\\ker(H-\\hbar\\omega(M+\\frac d2)I)$ only if those coefficients vanish. Hence the multiplicity is the number of $N\\in\\mathbb N^d$ with $\\sum_jn_j=M$, which is $\\binom{M+d-1}{d-1}$ by [F5]; for $d=3$ this reads $\\binom{M+2}{2}=(M+1)(M+2)/2$.",
          "inputs": [
            "2.2",
            "F3",
            "F5"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
