          "claim": "[(a) the formal solution] By [F2], $F\\in C^1$ with $F'=e^{y^2}$, so $u_2$ is $C^2$ with, using the product rule and [F1], $u_2'=e^{-y^2/2}(F'-yF)=e^{-y^2/2}\\bigl(e^{y^2}-yF\\bigr)$ and $u_2''=e^{-y^2/2}\\bigl(2ye^{y^2}-F-yF'-y(e^{y^2}-yF)\\bigr)=(y^2-1)u_2$ because the $e^{y^2}$ terms cancel. Since $(-\\partial_y^2+y^2)g_0=g_0$ by [F1], the equation $u''=(y^2-1)u$ is exactly the ground-state eigenvalue equation.",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "[(c) nonintegrability] For $y\\ge1$, $F(y)=\\int_0^ye^{t^2}dt\\ge\\int_{y-1}^ye^{t^2}dt\\ge e^{(y-1)^2}$ because $t\\ge y-1\\ge0$ on the interval and $t\\mapsto e^{t^2}$ is increasing there. Hence $|u_2(y)|^2=e^{-y^2}F(y)^2\\ge e^{-y^2}e^{2(y-1)^2}=e^{y^2-4y+2}$ for $y\\ge1$; the lower bound exceeds $1$ for $y\\ge4$, so $\\int_{\\mathbb R}|u_2|^2dy\\ge\\int_4^\\infty1\\,dy=+\\infty$ by [F3]. Hence $u_2\\notin L^2(\\mathbb R,dy)$.",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "[(b) the Wronskian] Using $g_0'=-yg_0$ from [F1] and the first formula of step 1.1, $W=g_0u_2'-g_0'u_2=\\pi^{-1/4}e^{-y^2/2}\\cdot e^{-y^2/2}(e^{y^2}-yF)+y\\pi^{-1/4}e^{-y^2/2}\\cdot e^{-y^2/2}F=\\pi^{-1/4}(1-yFe^{-y^2}+yFe^{-y^2})=\\pi^{-1/4}$, a nonzero constant; hence $g_0,u_2$ are linearly independent (a nontrivial linear combination $ag_0+bu_2$ with $W(ag_0+bu_2)=aW(g_0)+bW(u_2)$ cannot vanish identically unless $a=b=0$, since $W(g_0,g_0)=0$ and $W(g_0,u_2)=\\pi^{-1/4}$).",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "step": "3.1",
          "claim": "[(d) all $L^2$ solutions are multiples of $g_0$] Let $u$ be a $C^2$ solution of $u''=(y^2-1)u$ and put $W(u):=g_0u'-g_0'u$. Then $W(u)'=g_0u''-g_0''u=g_0(y^2-1)u-(y^2-1)g_0u=0$ using [F1], so $W(u)=c$ is constant. On each interval where $g_0>0$ (all of $\\mathbb R$ by [F1]) one has $(u/g_0)'=W(u)/g_0^2=c/g_0^2$, and since $1/g_0^2=\\pi^{1/2}e^{y^2}$ is continuous, [F3] integrated from $0$ to $y$ gives $u(y)=g_0(y)\\bigl(C+c\\pi^{1/2}F(y)\\bigr)$ for $C=u(0)/g_0(0)$. If $c\\ne0$ and $y\\ge1$, then $|u(y)|\\ge|c|\\pi^{1/2}g_0(y)e^{(y-1)^2}=|c|\\pi^{1/4}e^{y^2/2-2y+1}$ by the lower bound on $F$, which grows like $e^{y^2/2}$ and is not square-integrable by [F3]; so $u\\notin L^2$. Therefore if $u\\in L^2$ then $c=0$ and $u=Cg_0$.",
          "inputs": [
            "1.1",
            "F1",
            "F3"
          ]
